  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest surface analysis shows surface high\nslowly migrating off the mid-Atlantic coast this afternoon as\nbroad mid-level ridging continues to dominate over the southern\nplains and southeastern CONUS. After the seemingly unrelenting\nheat and humidity of the past few weeks, today's weather stands\nin stark contrast with highs in the low 80s and Tds dipping \ninto the mid to upper 50s.\n\nAs the high moves offshore, southerly to southwesterly flow will\nresume across the Carolinas bringing a gradual return of temps\ninto the 90s as well as a jump in humidity with Tds gradually\nworking back into the 70s. Mid-level subsidence aloft will keep\nprecipitation chances very subdued at least into tomorrow\ndespite the uptick in instability, although cannot completely\nrule out an isolated shower or two along typical sound and sea\nbreeze convergence zones. Shortwave approaches from the central\nplains on Thursday, weakening the ridge and aiding in a slightly\nmore favorable environment for isolated to widely scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Friday the high continues to shift offshore and\na front sweeps south through the Mid-Atlantic region. Ahead of\nthis front, continuous warm, moist advection in southerly flow \nwill help build PWATs to near 2\". This will bring scattered to \nnumerous sea breeze showers/tstorms back in the forecast. \nMeanwhile, despite the high shifting offshore, some residual \nridging should slow down the progression of the front as it \nmoves south. Where the front ends up stalling remains a point of\nuncertainty in the forecast with a handful of guidance now\nshowing the front getting hung up right across the middle of\neastern NC. Wherever the boundary sets up, along it training \nshowers/thunderstorms are possible, and a narrow swath of deep \nlayer shear right along the front would bring both a low end \nhydro and severe threat. The extent of the hydro and severe\nthreat are dependent on the frontal location and will continue\nto be monitored. As of this afternoon, hydrologic concerns\nremain the primary hazard but a low-end severe risk remains in\nthe cards.\n\nThe front lifts back north Saturday into Sunday as a shortwave\nmoves through the region. This shortwave will bring slightly\nbetter chances for showers and thunderstorms this weekend. \nShear doesn't look to be impressive, but ample moisture will \npromote a marginal hydro threat. There is some uncertainty with\nhow much instability is in play Saturday, as it is dependent on\ncloud cover and associated surface heating. In a low-end CAPE \noutcome, (500-1000J/kg) we would have long skinny CAPEs that \nsuggest just a hydro threat and less frequent lightning. \nMeanwhile a high- end CAPE outcome (2000-4000 J/kg) would bring \nmore pulse- like thunderstorms capable of producing some gusty \nwinds, frequent lightning, and heavy rainfall rates. Regardless \nof how Saturday plays out, there is higher confidence on a low-\nCAPE outcome Sunday. While heavy showers and some thunderstorms \nare possible Sunday with the shortwave, the severe threat \nappears to be more muted.",
